     "result_text_tail": "e and summary number and they all match. It also caught one real error (break-even for the SMB rise is just above twice the 2023 churn response, not at it) and several overstated claims. I've fixed all of them.\n\n**Choices I made in your place**\n- A deep deck of about 30\u201340 slides (it came to 40), because this is a CEO decision.\n- The briefing pack as the only material.\n- The audience is the CEO and leadership team approving the 2027 plan in December.\n- The navy theme.\n- Out of scope: repackaging and new plan tiers, a new-customer acquisition plan, and enterprise contracts that renew after 2027.\n- Planning assumptions:\n  - Enterprise renewals in 2027 are capped in the same 60% share as all enterprise ARR.\n  - SMB churn would react to a rise at least as it did after the 2023 rise.\n  - Retention in 2027 matches the last 12 months.\n  - The uncapped enterprise contracts also get 7%.\n\n**Gaps you should know about**\n- The pack has no new-customer bookings, so the $0.8M gap to the target is unchecked.\n- The board's churn figure (1.4% to 2.3%) isn't defined, and neither the customer-weighted nor the revenue-weighted average of segment churn reproduces it.\n- The pack has no survey sample sizes.\n\nNext, I can switch the theme or build a run of the SMB test design for the CEO. If you can get the 2026 bookings, I can also add them to close the target gap.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- palisade-pricing.pptx\n- palisade-pricing-sources.xlsx\n\nThe spec and its build script are in `./work/`."
    }
